WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.540
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome to the program
today, Daniel Pink, who's the author of five

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.769
New York Times bestsellers, including the
latest The Power of Regret How Looking Forward

00:00:09.769 --> 00:00:14.400
Moves US, I'm Sorry How Looking Backward Moves
US Forward.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.650
Published last month.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.160
Daniel, it's great to have you on today.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.140
Speaker 4: Hey, thanks for having me.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:25.821
Speaker 1: So I read the book with great interest,
and the book both confirms some of the intuition

00:00:25.821 --> 00:00:30.250
that we may have about regret and also and
also tells us that some of the intuitions

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:33.230
we have about regret are very, very wrong.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:39.260
Maybe to start with, can you talk a little
bit about when it comes to the idea of it's

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:44.030
just better to live without regrets, which
is a very general principle that a lot of

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:46.470
people assume is just accurate?

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:50.219
Can you talk a little bit about what aspects
of that make sense and maybe where it falls

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:51.219
short?

00:00:51.219 --> 00:00:57.129
Speaker 4: Well, it's I mean, it's a great
way to frame the question there.

00:00:57.129 --> 00:01:01.760
When we when we look, we have this idea that
we should never look backward and that we

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:07.590
should if we feel a spear of regret, we should
essentially ignore that.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:12.369
And that is actually colossally wrong headed
advice.

00:01:12.369 --> 00:01:16.710
What the research tells us, what 50 years
of science tells us is that everybody has

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:17.710
regrets.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.590
Regret is one of the most common emotions
that human beings have.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.310
It is ubiquitous in the human condition.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.540
It is actually part of our cognitive machinery.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.340
And so to say that I don't look backward and
I don't experience regret runs against the

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.390
science, it's just simply not how human beings
operate.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:39.700
So and the truth of the matter is that everybody,
you know, regret, regret makes us human.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.030
Nearly everybody experiences regret.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:46.299
I mean, literally, except for a very small
number of people with certain kinds of disorders.

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:49.240
Everybody experiences regret.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.380
And if we use it right, it's actually useful
to us.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:53.780
And this is the paradox of regret.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:57.560
This is sort of the puzzle of regret, which
is that this emotion that makes us feel bad

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.430
is ubiquitous in the human experience.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:00.680
So what why?

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:04.270
And the answer is because it helps us if we
deal with it right?

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:07.899
And we haven't been equipped to deal with
regret properly.

00:02:07.899 --> 00:02:10.020
We've been taught in some ways to ignore it.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.430
When that fails, we end up getting captured
by it.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:14.290
And what we should be doing is confronting
our regrets.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:18.930
Speaker 1: Yeah, you talk in the book about
and this doesn't seem unique to feelings of

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:19.930
regret.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:24.920
But in the book you talk about how if the
thoughts of regret become intrusive, if they

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:31.460
become sort of bottomless, if they become
repetitive, that they end up having a sort

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:32.880
of counterproductive impact.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:33.880
And it's not, it's not.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.040
It's not good.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.300
But that thinking about the regrets in particular
ways and using them is actually something

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:41.910
that that is a positive thing.

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.090
Can you talk a little bit about the difference
between that?

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:47.640
Because some people might say, Well, if I'm
not thinking about the regrets, then how can

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:48.910
I use them to my advantage?

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:51.550
Speaker 4: Well, so that's great.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.100
Another great, another great question.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:58.210
So it's really I think at a broader level,
we haven't been taught how to deal with negative

00:02:58.210 --> 00:02:59.390
emotions.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:02.810
I think our religious traditions actually
do a better job of equipping us to deal with

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:03.810
negative emotions.

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:06.520
I think secular society hasn't done a very
good job.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.940
And so what happens is that some of us ignore
negative emotions.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.950
We say, no, not real, got to be positive people.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.810
People put our fingers in the years.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.420
That's a bad idea.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:19.170
But because we haven't been equipped to deal
with them when we do, when they intrude, as

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.610
you say, they can feel overwhelming.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:25.970
And so we have to ask ourselves this question
about like, what are these negative feelings

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:26.970
for?

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.490
They're not for ignoring, they're not for
wallowing, they're for thinking about and

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:32.860
therefore thinking about, as you say, in a
particular way.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:38.700
And there's a certain process by which we
can think about our emotions and arrest that

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:44.010
march toward rumination, arrest that march
toward getting sucked into the into the vortex.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.770
And it's actually relatively simple.

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:51.660
And when we do that, the evidence is overwhelming
that that reckoning with our regrets leaning

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:56.440
into our regrets in some ways can help us
become better negotiators, better strategies,

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:59.460
better problem solvers, better parents and
find greater meaning in life.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:03.290
Speaker 1: You know, it's interesting that
our interview is today and yesterday.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:09.600
It just so happened that during these Supreme
Court confirmation hearings, particular sentences

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.370
that the nominee Judge Jackson had assigned
in the past were a topic of much discussion.

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:20.290
And she was asked numerous times Do you regret
the sentence you gave to this particular individual

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:23.340
who went on to re-offend or whatever the case
may be?

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:27.430
And one of the interesting things about that
framework is that it's sort of like a results

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:33.320
based regret where the time that that a decision
is made, you don't have the information as

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:35.000
to what subsequently happens.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:39.010
And the question was based on what happened
after you decided.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:40.540
Do you regret what you did?

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:46.570
And Daniel Kahneman has written about this
and in the context of it being sort of a cognitive

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:48.760
limitation that we sometimes experience?

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:54.100
Can you talk a little bit about results oriented
thoughts about regrets?

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.780
Speaker 4: Yeah.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:05:02.890
I mean, I don't want to legitimize those questions
about sentencing as as serious intellectual

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:04.430
inquiries
Speaker 1: agreed 100

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:05.660
Speaker 4: percent.

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:07.290
They were, they were.

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:13.070
Yeah, it was basically performative grandstanding
and dog whistles.

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:14.070
Absolutely.

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:15.070
I believe in leaving that.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:19.080
Aside from leaving that aside for a moment,
and I know, I know that you agree with that.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.560
Yeah, yeah.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:24.580
I mean, this is actually there are a couple
of ways at this.

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:26.190
One of them is as we were talking about before.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.910
That's a way to arrest the march toward rumination.

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:34.850
That is what you need to evaluate is a decision
itself, not the not what happened subsequently,

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:37.510
because the decision itself is something you
had control over.

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:40.890
What happened subsequently is something you
don't have control over.

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:44.330
And so what I found in talking to people about
their regrets is they were actually much less

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:50.340
outcomes than I would have expected that they
were actually scrutinizing that, especially

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.000
when it came to a certain kinds of regrets.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.970
They were much more willing to scrutinize
the decision itself and were less wedded to

00:05:57.970 --> 00:05:58.970
the outcome.

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:01.120
Now all of us have that.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.330
All of us have that cognitive glitch.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:10.060
But but what's important to understand with
with regret is that when we evaluate our choices

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:15.460
and when we try to extract lessons from them,
we should be evaluating the context of the

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:19.220
decision, what we knew, what situation we
were in.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:23.210
Who in some ways, who we were as a person
at that moment.

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:25.280
And because that's something we have control
over.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:28.931
But the outcome itself, we don't always have
control over, although the outcome does, you

00:06:28.931 --> 00:06:30.050
know, can give us clues.

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:36.600
Speaker 1: You in the book, you categorize
regrets into sort of certain buckets.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:43.080
And in that context, you did this world regret
survey of 15000 people in more than 100 countries.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:49.580
Can you talk about the categories of regret
that seem to be the most impactful for people

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:51.440
when they look back on their lives?

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:52.440
Speaker 4: Yeah.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:56.650
I mean, what I found in collecting regrets
from all over the world, as you mentioned,

00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:02.630
we're now up to over 19000 regrets from over
from 109 countries.

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:03.930
It's unbelievable.

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:07.889
And what I found, which is, to my surprise,
is that over and over again, people had the

00:07:07.889 --> 00:07:09.340
same core regrets.

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:10.980
They regretted the same things.

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:13.550
And these regrets are less about the domains
of people's lives.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:14.889
They were less about, Oh, I have a career
regret.

00:07:14.889 --> 00:07:16.419
I have an education regret.

00:07:16.419 --> 00:07:18.330
I mean, superficially, that's what they were.

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:22.581
But deep down, there were four things that
people seem to regret the most, and I'll go

00:07:22.581 --> 00:07:23.610
through them very quickly.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:27.710
One of them, or what I call foundation regrets
foundation regrets are if only I'd done the

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:28.710
work.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:32.260
And these are regrets about small decisions
people make about health, about finances,

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:38.740
about education early in life that accumulate
to, you know, sometimes very grave consequences

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:40.180
later on.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:45.270
So things about especially things like not
saving enough money, spending too much money,

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:46.770
smoking those kinds of things.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:50.510
Second one are boldness regrets, which are
if only I'd taken the chance.

00:07:50.510 --> 00:07:53.180
These are regrets people have where they're
at a juncture in their life.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:57.150
They can play it safe, they can take the chance
and they don't take the chance and they often

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:58.150
regret it.

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:03.610
And it's everything from traveling to speaking
up to asking somebody out on a date to starting

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:04.610
a business.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:07.300
And what's interesting about those, David,
is that.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:13.730
There were people who acted boldly and it
didn't work out.

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:18.820
And many of them said, I don't love that,
but I'm cool with that because I took the

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:22.720
chance, so that's a good example of what you
were talking about before people being, to

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:25.000
my surprise, being less outcomes than I would
have expected.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:28.300
Third category, if only I'd done the right
thing.

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:30.850
These are people who again are at a juncture.

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:31.850
They can do the right thing.

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:33.540
They can do the wrong thing, to do the wrong
thing.

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:34.570
They regret it.

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:36.339
Bullying, marital infidelity.

00:08:36.339 --> 00:08:37.520
Other kinds of cheating.

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:42.420
And finally, our connection regrets, which
are if only had reached out, which are about

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:47.839
relationships, not mostly romantic relationships,
just the full spectrum of relationships we

00:08:47.839 --> 00:08:52.500
have in our lives that if they come apart
and we don't do anything about it, we end

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:53.680
up regretting it.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:09:00.400
And and what's amazing to me in this research
is that is how universal these four regrets

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:01.400
were.

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:07.760
I mean, irrespective of, you know, of of nationality,
especially pretty much irrespective of gender

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:10.550
and to some extent, even irrespective of age.

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:16.200
Speaker 1: Are there certain cultures or countries
that just have a lower level of regret in

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:17.200
your estimation?

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:18.450
Speaker 4: I don't know.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:19.880
That's an interesting question.

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:21.380
I tried to get at that.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:26.460
I tried to get at that question in in a I
did another piece of that to the the world

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:27.460
regrets.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:30.520
Everyone's a piece of qualitative research
that as I was essentially collecting stories,

