00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:52.080
Can also lead to regret.

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:56.950
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to touch on in
terms of takeaways for one's own thinking

00:13:56.950 --> 00:14:00.040
and actions and sort of life.

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:06.170
One principle that I got from the book maybe,
rightly, maybe wrongly, is in general, there

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:13.580
seems to be more regret for things not done
than for things done very, very broadly speaking.

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:18.180
Is that true and what other kind of general
principles that one can apply, do you think

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:19.240
are important?

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:21.820
Speaker 4: It is.

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:23.830
What you're saying is very true.

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:28.490
I mean, that think that that's a claim that
I can make without with very little hesitation

00:14:28.490 --> 00:14:31.470
based on the existing research and based on
my own research and the quantitative survey,

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:36.170
that was a very, very clear finding, particularly
when it comes to age when we're younger, we

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:41.230
tend to have equal regrets of of of action,
things I did and inaction what I didn't do

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:42.500
as we age.

00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:44.070
Overwhelmingly, it's regrets of inaction.

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:48.149
And I think that the takeaway there is that
in general in life, we should have a slight

00:14:48.149 --> 00:14:54.130
bias for action that that in some ways we're
generally over indexed on risk and we're over

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:56.589
indexed on feelings of awkwardness.

00:14:56.589 --> 00:15:00.580
So we think that asking somebody out on a
date has a high level of risk when it actually

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:01.580
doesn't.

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:05.620
We think that reaching out to a friend who's
drifted apart, it has high levels of awkwardness,

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:07.010
but it really doesn't.

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:10.670
We think this is a little bit closer to the
line.

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:16.570
But but we sometimes overindex on how much
risk it is to start a business.

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:20.750
And and so I think that a takeaway is that
we should have a in general in life a bias

00:15:20.750 --> 00:15:26.570
for action because action is also a way that
it's also a way that we learn things.

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:31.149
So I think sometimes we plan too much and
act too little, which we feel like in order

00:15:31.149 --> 00:15:35.410
to act, we have to plan out everything when
in fact, acting itself is a form of planning

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:36.410
and understanding.

00:15:36.410 --> 00:15:37.769
So I think that's one big takeaway.

00:15:37.769 --> 00:15:42.690
The other big takeaway is, I think in terms
of how we reckon with our regrets is that

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:47.000
we should recognize that when we feel regret
it is utterly human and that when we do that,

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:49.829
we should treat ourselves with kindness rather
than contempt.

00:15:49.829 --> 00:15:53.149
Recognize that it's part of the human condition.

00:15:53.149 --> 00:15:57.490
We should talk about our regrets and use language
to make sense of our regrets, and we should

00:15:57.490 --> 00:16:01.529
explicitly stop and try to extract a lesson
from those regrets.

00:16:01.529 --> 00:16:07.570
So if we do that, if we say, you know, this
is this is this is what I regret.

00:16:07.570 --> 00:16:08.570
It's totally normal.

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:10.570
I'm going to treat myself with kindness.

00:16:10.570 --> 00:16:12.160
Let me try to make sense of it.

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:15.400
Let me draw a lesson from it and let me apply
that lesson next time.

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:19.260
Then it's an incredibly productive and even
transformative emotion.

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:24.830
Speaker 1: The book is the power of Regret
How Looking Backward Moves US Forward.

00:16:24.830 --> 00:16:27.910
We've been speaking with the book's author,
Daniel Pink.

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:29.010
Really appreciate your time today.

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:30.600
Speaker 4: Thanks for having me.

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:31.650
It was a pleasure.

